---
// Elemental reactions deep dive — targets "vaporize vs melt", "elemental
// mastery calculator", "reaction damage" long-tail queries.
//
// Element names in the two tables are drawn with the element logos instead of
// spelled out; the name survives in each icon's alt text, so the tables stay
// readable to crawlers and screen readers.
const ELEMENT_NAME: Record<string, string> = {
  pyro: 'Pyro',
  hydro: 'Hydro',
  electro: 'Electro',
  cryo: 'Cryo',
  anemo: 'Anemo',
  geo: 'Geo',
  dendro: 'Dendro',
};

const elIcon = (el: string) =>
  `<img src="/images/element-${el}.webp" alt="${ELEMENT_NAME[el] ?? el}" width="120" height="120" class="inline-block rounded-full bg-white object-contain p-px shadow-sm ring-1 ring-black/5 align-middle h-5 w-5" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />`;

/** "Hydro on Pyro" — the element that lands, then the aura it reacts with. */
const elOn = (applied: string, aura: string) =>
  `<span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5">${elIcon(applied)}<span class="text-xs text-[var(--muted)]">on</span>${elIcon(aura)}</span>`;

/** "Pyro + Electro" */
const elPair = (els: string[]) =>
  `<span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5">${els.map((e) => elIcon(e)).join('<span class="text-xs text-[var(--muted)]">+</span>')}</span>`;

/** "Dendro core + Electro" */
const elCore = (el: string) =>
  `<span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5">${elIcon('dendro')}<span class="text-xs text-[var(--muted)]">core</span><span class="text-xs text-[var(--muted)]">+</span>${elIcon(el)}</span>`;

/** "Anemo + absorbed element" */
const elAbsorb = () =>
  `<span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5">${elIcon('anemo')}<span class="text-xs text-[var(--muted)]">+ absorbed element</span></span>`;
---

<section id="reactions" class="page-shell section-pad scroll-mt-20 border-t border-[var(--line)]">
  <h2 class="text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-[var(--text)]">Elemental reactions explained</h2>
  <p class="mt-3 max-w-[65ch] text-[var(--muted)]">
    Reactions fall into three families that scale completely differently — pick the one your team actually triggers.
  </p>

  <!-- Amplifying reactions -->
  <div class="mt-8 panel p-6">
    <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--text)]">Amplifying reactions: Vaporize &amp; Melt</h3>
    <p class="mt-3 text-[var(--muted)]">
      These <strong class="text-[var(--text)]">multiply your entire hit</strong>, including all your ATK, CRIT and DMG
      Bonus — which is why Vaporize and Melt carries are the game’s highest single-hit damage dealers. The multiplier
      depends on which element triggers:
    </p>
    <div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full min-w-[520px] text-left text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr class="border-b border-[var(--line)] text-[var(--muted)]">
            <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium">Reaction</th>
            <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium">Trigger order</th>
            <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium">Multiplier</th>
            <th class="py-2 font-medium">Example</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="text-[var(--text)]">
          <tr class="border-b border-[var(--line)]">
            <td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Vaporize</td>
            <td class="py-2.5 pr-4" set:html={elOn('hydro', 'pyro')}></td>
            <td class="tnum py-2.5 pr-4 text-forest-600">2.0×</td>
            <td class="py-2.5">Reverse Vaporize</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-[var(--line)]">
            <td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Vaporize</td>
            <td class="py-2.5 pr-4" set:html={elOn('pyro', 'hydro')}></td>
            <td class="tnum py-2.5 pr-4 text-forest-600">1.5×</td>
            <td class="py-2.5">Forward Vaporize</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-[var(--line)]">
            <td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Melt</td>
            <td class="py-2.5 pr-4" set:html={elOn('pyro', 'cryo')}></td>
            <td class="tnum py-2.5 pr-4 text-forest-600">2.0×</td>
            <td class="py-2.5">Forward Melt</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Melt</td>
            <td class="py-2.5 pr-4" set:html={elOn('cryo', 'pyro')}></td>
            <td class="tnum py-2.5 pr-4 text-forest-600">1.5×</td>
            <td class="py-2.5">Reverse Melt</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <details class="accordion mt-4">
      <summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-3 text-sm font-semibold text-forest-600 transition-colors hover:text-forest-700">
        <span>How Elemental Mastery scales it</span>
        <svg class="h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-[var(--muted)] transition-transform group-open:rotate-45" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M12 5v14M5 12h14" /></svg>
      </summary>
      <p class="mt-3 text-[var(--muted)]">
        Elemental Mastery scales the multiplier further by
        <span class="tnum text-[var(--text)]">2.78 × EM ÷ (EM + 1400)</span>. At 100 EM that’s about +18.5% on top of the
        base multiplier, at 200 EM about +35%, but by 400 EM the returns have flattened to about +62% — the first 100 EM
        is worth nearly as much as the next 300. That is why reaction carries aim for roughly 100-200 EM and spend the
        rest of their budget on CRIT.
      </p>
    </details>
  </div>

  <!-- Transformative reactions -->
  <div class="mt-6 panel p-6">
    <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--text)]">Transformative reactions: Overload, Bloom &amp; more</h3>
    <p class="mt-3 text-[var(--muted)]">
      These deal a <strong class="text-[var(--text)]">separate, fixed hit</strong> that ignores ATK, talent multiplier
      and CRIT entirely. Their damage depends only on the triggering character’s <strong class="text-[var(--text)]">level</strong>,
      their <strong class="text-[var(--text)]">Elemental Mastery</strong>, and the enemy’s RES. The formula is:
    </p>
    <p class="tnum mt-3 rounded-[10px] bg-[var(--surface-2)] p-4 text-center text-sm text-[var(--text)]">
      Transformative Damage = Level Multiplier × Reaction Coefficient × (1 + EM bonus) × RES multiplier
    </p>
    <div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full min-w-[520px] text-left text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr class="border-b border-[var(--line)] text-[var(--muted)]">
            <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium">Reaction</th>
            <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium">Coefficient</th>
            <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium">Elements</th>
            <th class="py-2 font-medium">Typical build</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="text-[var(--text)]">
          <tr class="border-b border-[var(--line)]"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Overload</td><td class="tnum py-2.5 pr-4">2.75</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4" set:html={elPair(['pyro', 'electro'])}></td><td class="py-2.5">Full EM</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-[var(--line)]"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Superconduct</td><td class="tnum py-2.5 pr-4">1.5</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4" set:html={elPair(['cryo', 'electro'])}></td><td class="py-2.5">Support only</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-[var(--line)]"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Electro-Charged</td><td class="tnum py-2.5 pr-4">2.0</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4" set:html={elPair(['hydro', 'electro'])}></td><td class="py-2.5">Full EM</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-[var(--line)]"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Swirl</td><td class="tnum py-2.5 pr-4">0.6</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4" set:html={elAbsorb()}></td><td class="py-2.5">Full EM</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-[var(--line)]"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Shatter</td><td class="tnum py-2.5 pr-4">3.0</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Heavy hit on a Frozen target</td><td class="py-2.5">Physical carry</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-[var(--line)]"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Burning</td><td class="tnum py-2.5 pr-4">0.25</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4" set:html={elPair(['dendro', 'pyro'])}></td><td class="py-2.5">Support</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-[var(--line)]"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Bloom</td><td class="tnum py-2.5 pr-4">2.0</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4" set:html={elPair(['hydro', 'dendro'])}></td><td class="py-2.5">Full EM</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-[var(--line)]"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Hyperbloom</td><td class="tnum py-2.5 pr-4">3.0</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4" set:html={elCore('electro')}></td><td class="py-2.5">Full EM</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Burgeon</td><td class="tnum py-2.5 pr-4">3.0</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4" set:html={elCore('pyro')}></td><td class="py-2.5">Full EM</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <details class="accordion mt-4">
      <summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-3 text-sm font-semibold text-forest-600 transition-colors hover:text-forest-700">
        <span>Level &amp; EM scaling numbers</span>
        <svg class="h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-[var(--muted)] transition-transform group-open:rotate-45" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M12 5v14M5 12h14" /></svg>
      </summary>
      <p class="mt-3 text-[var(--muted)]">
        The level multiplier at character level 90 is <span class="tnum text-[var(--text)]">1446.85</span> (at 80 it is
        1077.44 — a ~34% jump from 80 to 90). EM scales by <span class="tnum text-[var(--text)]">16 × EM ÷ (EM + 2000)</span>.
        Because these reactions ignore CRIT, a Hyperbloom trigger wants <em>only</em> EM and level — a 900 EM level-90
        Hyperbloom deals roughly 23,000 per seed against a 10%-RES enemy, which is why Hyperbloom teams are the cheapest
        high-damage option in the game.
      </p>
    </details>

    <!-- Hyperbloom worked example — targets "hyperbloom damage formula" -->
    <h4 class="mt-8 text-lg font-semibold text-[var(--text)]">Hyperbloom damage formula (worked example)</h4>
    <p class="mt-3 text-[var(--muted)]">
      Hyperbloom triggers when an <strong class="text-[var(--text)]">Electro</strong> hit strikes a Dendro core created
      by Hydro + Dendro. It is a fixed reaction instance — it ignores ATK, talent multiplier and CRIT:
    </p>
    <p class="tnum mt-3 rounded-[10px] bg-[var(--surface-2)] p-4 text-center text-sm text-[var(--text)]">
      Hyperbloom Damage = 3.0 × LevelMult × (1 + 16 × EM ÷ (EM + 2000)) × RES multiplier
    </p>
    <div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full min-w-[520px] text-left text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr class="border-b border-[var(--line)] text-[var(--muted)]">
            <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium">Term</th>
            <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium">Value (Lv 90, 900 EM, 10% RES)</th>
            <th class="py-2 font-medium">Source</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="text-[var(--text)]">
          <tr class="border-b border-[var(--line)]"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Reaction coefficient</td><td class="tnum py-2.5 pr-4">3.0</td><td class="py-2.5">Hyperbloom</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-[var(--line)]"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Level multiplier</td><td class="tnum py-2.5 pr-4">1446.85</td><td class="py-2.5">Character level 90</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-[var(--line)]"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">EM bonus</td><td class="tnum py-2.5 pr-4">+496.6%</td><td class="py-2.5">16 × 900 ÷ (900 + 2000)</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-[var(--line)]"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">EM multiplier</td><td class="tnum py-2.5 pr-4">×5.966</td><td class="py-2.5">1 + EM bonus</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-[var(--line)]"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">RES multiplier</td><td class="tnum py-2.5 pr-4">×0.90</td><td class="py-2.5">1 − 0.10</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="py-2.5 pr-4 font-semibold">Per-seed damage</td><td class="tnum py-2.5 pr-4 font-semibold text-forest-600">≈ 23,300</td><td class="py-2.5">3.0 × 1446.85 × 5.966 × 0.90</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--muted)]">
      Two consequences follow from the formula. First, <strong class="text-[var(--text)]">level is a real upgrade</strong>:
      going from 80 to 90 lifts the level multiplier by ~34%, so the trigger character (Kuki Shinobu, Raiden, etc.) should
      be levelled to 90. Second, <strong class="text-[var(--text)]">no amount of ATK or CRIT changes the number</strong> —
      EM is the only offensive stat, so the trigger runs an EM weapon and an EM / EM / EM artifact line.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- Quicken reactions -->
  <div class="mt-6 panel p-6">
    <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--text)]">Quicken reactions: Aggravate &amp; Spread</h3>
    <p class="mt-3 text-[var(--muted)]">
      Quicken adds a flat damage bonus that still <em>benefits from CRIT and DMG Bonus</em> — so Aggravate/Spread carries build a normal CRIT set.
    </p>
    <details class="accordion mt-3">
      <summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-3 text-sm font-semibold text-forest-600 transition-colors hover:text-forest-700">
        <span>Why the two Dendro families build differently</span>
        <svg class="h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-[var(--muted)] transition-transform group-open:rotate-45" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M12 5v14M5 12h14" /></svg>
      </summary>
      <p class="mt-3 text-[var(--muted)]">
        This is the single most common build mistake with Dendro teams: applying Bloom’s full-EM logic to a Quicken
        carry. Practical rule: <strong class="text-[var(--text)]">Quicken carries build CRIT + EM sands; Bloom-family
        triggers build pure EM.</strong> Identify which reaction your team actually triggers before farming artifacts.
      </p>
    </details>
  </div>
</section>
